BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dinner with Matthew Campbell
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260707T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260707T203000
UID:bm-5427920260707@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
DESCRIPTION:[Dinner at Cirillo's]\nMatthew Campbell will be joining us for
  dinner at Cirillo's\, and we hope you can make it\, too!\nJoin us for a d
 elicious meal\, and a discussion about The Man Who Stole the Gods\, Matthe
 w's new piece of investigative journalism looking at an immense conspiracy
  centred around the looting of Cambodian treasures during the country's br
 utal genocide.\nAbout the Event:\nat Cirillo's\, 118 The Esplanade\nDoors 
 open at 6pm\nCash bar will be open while dinner is being prepared.\nIt's a
  chance to mingle\, meet the author\, and/or watch the meal being cooked.\
 nAround 6:30 everyone will be invited to take their seats\, and dinner wil
 l be served.\n\nThroughout the dinner Matthew will be checking in at diff
 erent tables and speaking with attendees.\nFollowing dinner Matthew will t
 alk about his book\, the process of writing it\, and answer any questions.
 \nEach participant will have a chance to get their book signed during dess
 ert\, if they haven't had a chance to do so already.\nTicket includes:\n1 
 copy of the The Man Who Stole the Gods\nand\n1 three-course dinner\nPlease
  reach out about vegetarian options or dietary restrictions\, and we'll do
  our best to accommodate you.\nAbout the Book:\nFrom the Killing Fields of
  Cambodia to the gilded halls of New York’s Metropolitan Museum of Art\,
  a tale of stolen treasures and the battle to reclaim a nation’s soul.\n
 Amidst the chaos of Cambodia's brutal genocide\, a new crime wave emerged
 —one that would sweep across borders and entangle the world's most prest
 igious art institutions. Priceless treasures of the ancient Khmer Empire\,
  the civilization that produced Angkor Wat\, vanished from sacred temples\
 , looted by smugglers and trafficked into the hands of elite collectors. A
 t the center of it all was a man named Douglas Latchford.\nKnown later as 
 "Dynamite Doug" for the ruthless methods used to extract statues from temp
 le ruins\, Latchford orchestrated one of history's most audacious cultural
  heists. From dusty Cambodian villages to the glittering auction houses of
  London and New York and institutions like the Met\, he played a double ga
 me—presenting himself as an expert on Khmer art while secretly flooding 
 the market with stolen antiquities.\nIn\nThe Man Who Stole the Gods\n\, aw
 ard-winning journalist Matthew Campbell unravels the gripping story of Lat
 chford's criminal enterprise\, and a global conspiracy of greed and collus
 ion—one that involves some of the world's most powerful museums and coll
 ectors.\n\nA masterful blend of true crime\, history\, and investigative j
 ournalism\,\nThe Man Who Stole the Gods\nis the definitive account of one 
 man's greed\, an industry's complicity\, and the fight to expose the truth
  and restore stolen treasures to their rightful home.\nAbout the Author:\n
 Matthew Campbell is an award-winning reporter for Bloomberg Businessweek a
 nd the author of\nThe Man Who Stole the Gods: A True Story of War\, Obsess
 ion\, and a Global Art Conspiracy\n. His previous book\, Dead in the Water
 —co-authored with Kit Chellel—was selected as a Book of the Year by\nT
 he Economist\n\, the\nFinancial Times\n\, and\nThe Times\n.\nMatt has repo
 rted from more than twenty-five countries on crime\, corruption\, terroris
 m\, economics\, and the environment. His work has earned some of journalis
 m’s highest honors\, including awards from the Gerald Loeb Foundation\, 
 the Overseas Press Club\, the National Press Club\, SOPA\, and SABEW for b
 oth feature and investigative reporting. A 2025 Jonathan Logan Family Foun
 dation Fellow at New America\, Matt lives in Singapore with his family.
LOCATION:Cirillo's Academy
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Blue Life Book Launch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T190000
UID:bm-5808420260708@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/7600763/Website2/
 Events/2026_07_08.png?cb=1783176624
DESCRIPTION:[at Ben McNally Books]\nJoin us at Ben McNally Books\, in part
 nership with Civil Sector Press\, to celebrate the launch of\nBlue Life\n\
 , a novel by Mundy McLaughlin\nAbout the Book:\nA lifetime in the making\
 , Blue Life (Civil Sector Press\, 2026) is the debut novel from Canadian a
 uthor\nMundy McLaughlin.\nSet in late 1970s New York City\, Blue Life foll
 ows the intertwined paths of Luna\, an\nart student and Didi\, an aspiring
  actress who meet as performers in the legendary “Go-Go” dancing\nscen
 e across the water in New Jersey.\nBonding on their shared experiences\, L
 una and Didi form an instant connection\, but while Luna’s path\nascends
  through her work as a burgeoning artist in New York City’s famed East V
 illage\, Didi’s life spirals\ndownwards. Their divergent\, but connected
  paths test both their friendship and their loyalty to one\nanother.\n\nMc
 Laughlin’s evocative writing offers a front row seat to Luna’s turbule
 nt journey through the seedy\nNew Jersey go-go scene and New York’s vibr
 ant punk and dance clubs. Her empathy and sensitivity to\nthe turbulence o
 f Didi’s topsy turvy life gives Blue Life momentum and her ability to ev
 oke the glitz and\nglam of NYC hotspots like Studio 54\, CBGBs\, the Mudd 
 Club\, and the Roxy with remarkable detail plays\nbeautifully off the vibr
 ancy and grit of the East Village art world of the time.\nBlue Life is a c
 ompassionate and empathetic look at friendship and loyalty set against the
  substantial\ncultural and social changes of late ‘70s and early ‘80s 
 America - a unique time when everything felt like\nit should be possible.\
 nMundy McLaughlin’s debut work of fiction offers a loving\, but unflinch
 ing look\nbehind the perceived boundaries of friendship and the limits of 
 sexual power with a cast of memorable\ncharacters whose individual stories
  stay with the reader long after the story is over.\nAbout the Author:\nBo
 rn in Toronto\, Ontario\, from 1980 to 1986 Mundy McLaughin was a member o
 f Group Material\, a\nNew York City-based collective that organized exhibi
 ts that illuminated social and political themes.\nHer photographic and scu
 lptural work examined the relationship between fantasy and reality in\nNor
 th American culture\, gender relations and commercialism. Mundy left New Y
 ork and was called\nto the Ontario bar in 1992. Mundy eventually became we
 ll regarded in the field of workplace human\nrights and equity issues. She
  holds degrees in Common and Civil Law from McGill University and a\nMaste
 rs in Law from Osgoode Hall Law School. She has also completed a Masters o
 f Fine Arts in\nCreative Writing at Lesley University in Cambridge\, Massa
 chusetts. Having spent time in New York\nCity\, Montreal and Ottawa\, Mund
 y now lives in rural Ontario. Blue Life is her first novel.
LOCATION:Ben McNally Books
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
